1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,673
Oil helps your carrots.

2
00:00:01,673 --> 00:00:03,200
Heat can cost your
peppers.

3
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:04,724
One pan.

4
00:00:04,724 --> 00:00:05,867
Two opposite things

5
00:00:05,867 --> 00:00:07,354
happening to your
veggies.

6
00:00:07,954 --> 00:00:10,368
Bright veggies like
carrots are full of

7
00:00:10,368 --> 00:00:12,560
plant pigments called
carotenoids.

8
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,810
Your body turns them
into vitamin A.

9
00:00:15,510 --> 00:00:16,609
Here's the catch.

10
00:00:16,609 --> 00:00:18,159
Those pigments get
absorbed

11
00:00:18,159 --> 00:00:19,544
better with a little
fat.

12
00:00:20,144 --> 00:00:21,545
Think of it like a ride.

13
00:00:21,545 --> 00:00:23,585
The pigments travel
along with the oil.

14
00:00:24,185 --> 00:00:26,492
So a splash of oil isn't
just for flavor.

15
00:00:27,092 --> 00:00:29,562
Same story for vitamins
E and K,

16
00:00:29,562 --> 00:00:31,498
in spinach and broccoli.

17
00:00:31,498 --> 00:00:32,637
Fat helps them in.

18
00:00:33,537 --> 00:00:34,808
Now the flip side.

19
00:00:34,808 --> 00:00:37,278
Peppers, broccoli and
tomatoes are

20
00:00:37,278 --> 00:00:38,830
packed with vitamin C.

21
00:00:39,430 --> 00:00:41,582
Vitamin C dissolves in
water,

22
00:00:41,582 --> 00:00:44,380
and cooking can lower
how much is left.

23
00:00:45,080 --> 00:00:47,986
Microwaving and steaming
can lower it too,

24
00:00:47,986 --> 00:00:49,675
so it's not just the
pan.

25
00:00:50,275 --> 00:00:52,240
That's why raw fruit and
veg

26
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:53,866
top the vitamin C lists.

27
00:00:54,766 --> 00:00:56,812
So sautéing isn't good
or bad.

28
00:00:56,812 --> 00:00:57,685
It's a trade.

29
00:00:58,285 --> 00:00:59,839
This isn't a diet rule.

30
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:01,676
It's just what your pan
is doing.

31
00:01:03,176 --> 00:01:05,945
The Pan Theory: carrots
want a little oil.

32
00:01:05,945 --> 00:01:07,666
Peppers can keep more C
raw.

